{"obligations":{"reduction":{"status":"not_written","quote":"Then $g(x) > -x$ and (3) rewrites as","witness":"The required conclusion $g(t)\ge 0$ for every $t>0$ is not proved. The text would need to iterate (3) or (4) to obtain $f^n(t)=t+n g(t)>0$ for every integer $n\ge0$, which rules out $g(t)<0$. The displayed bound $g(x)>-x$ alone is insufficient.","load_bearing":"Nonnegativity of every difference is part of the structural reduction and is later needed to classify the range of $g$."},"lower_bound":{"status":"written_and_valid","quote":null,"witness":null,"load_bearing":null},"upper_bound":{"status":"not_written","quote":"Thus $V$ contains **at most one** positive number.  Consequently, either  

* (i) $g\equiv 0$ (constant), which gives $f(x)=x$, a solution; or  
* (ii) there is $c>0$ such that $V \subseteq \{0, c\}$ with both values attainable.","witness":"The argument has shown only that two distinct positive values cannot occur; it has not written an argument excluding negative values of $g$. To cover all cases it must prove that $g(t)<0$ is impossible by iterating the orbit until $t+n g(t)\le0$. Without that step, the asserted dichotomy and all subsequent level-set arguments omit a case.","load_bearing":"Sections 4–6 assume every point lies in $S_0\cup S_c$. Thus the claimed constancy of $g$ depends on the unwritten exclusion of negative differences."}},"S_valid":1,"wrong_answer_stated":false,"notes":"The stated answer and the QM–AM–GM verification are correct. The necessity proof has an unfilled negative-difference case before its range dichotomy."}
